{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs at exact clean head fd79bd7fd208a322af7c85d40c5cdb3abdeb4f68, succeeding fe400c05. Companion to the cmd/gs artifact f68313f3 and the internal/statusview artifact 85d860fd at this same head; all three must be co-signed together.\n\nFOUR NEW REFERENCE PAGES, one per new subcommand: work.md, artifacts.md, inspect.md, reviews.md. status.md gains a section, \"Selecting instead of dumping\", saying plainly that --json is the right answer when you need whole event identifiers and the wrong one when you need a subset, and pointing at the four. provenance.md gains a section distinguishing its own per-event walk from the population-wide anchor question that --reaches answers, which is the distinction the request's block 4 turns on and which would otherwise look like two commands doing the same thing. architecture.md records the new CLI reach.\n\nTHE CITATION DEFECT, WHICH IS THE PART OF THIS ARTIFACT MOST WORTH READING. This head was written against c7054dcd and waited while four merges landed. All five pages it adds or edits cited the cmd/gs artifact standing at c80fbc61, and one of those merges retired it. A page written today citing an artifact retired yesterday is stale from the moment it exists, which is the exact opposite of what a citation is for: the point of resting documentation on the artifacts for the behaviour it describes is that the page flares when the behaviour moves, and a page that arrives already flaring teaches the reader to ignore flares.\n\nI found it because the implementation named the risk rather than hiding it — its report said to re-check those two citations were still live before filing — and then I checked rather than trusting either direction. The cmd/gs one was retired; the internal/statusview one was still live. All five now cite a1055e9d.\n\nI audited every citation the head adds, not only the two named: there are exactly two distinct ones, and both are now live. I also checked what the head does NOT touch, because it would be easy to over-reach here: status.md, provenance.md and architecture.md carry other citations that are retired, and those are untouched and byte-identical to main. They predate this head, repointing them is separate work with its own decisions about which successor each should follow, and quietly rewriting a page's whole basis list while adding a section to it would hide a real change inside an unrelated one.\n\nWHAT THE PAGES SAY THAT A REVIEWER SHOULD CHECK AGAINST THE CODE. That --reaches P excludes artifacts recorded at P itself. That gs reviews exits non-zero when the queue is not quiet. That state defaults to live and that the default is unchanged behaviour. And the mapping from each runbook jq block to its command — including, honestly, that two blocks have no command and why.\n\nAN OPERATIONAL NOTE THAT BELONGS IN THE DOCS' AUDIENCE'S HANDS. The running resident refuses the new state field, so a selector query against an old resident falls back to a full verified local rebuild and says so. Whoever merges this must restart the resident and adapters on the new binary, or every --state query pays a whole-log verification.\n\nGATES at this exact head: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok, internal/docset among them and uncached, which is the gate that reads these pages and their citations; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0; go vet exit 0; go build exit 0; gofmt empty; git diff --check clean; worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"fd79bd7fd208a322af7c85d40c5cdb3abdeb4f68","path":"docs"}}